84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (The Browser History Manager is a modern web-based application that simulates the core navigation engine and history record system of production web browsers. It is an interactive Data Structures and Algorithms \(DSA\) project built strictly with Vanilla JavaScript, HTML5, and CSS3 for client-side execution without any heavy frontend frameworks or external runtime dependencies.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Two custom Stack instances \(Back Stack and Forward Stack\) manage browser-like bidirectional navigation adhering to the Last In, First Out \(LIFO\) paradigm. A custom Doubly Linked List maintains the complete chronological timeline of visited pages, where each page is an individual node containing bidirectional links, enabling dynamic pointer updates and forward/backward traversals. A custom HashMap with separate chaining collision resolution stores unique URLs as keys along with metadata \(page title, total visit count, and last visited timestamp\) to support instantaneous O\(1\) average lookup and searching.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (The user enters a URL in an interactive browser bar; the application updates the Stacks, appends the entry to the Doubly Linked List, and synchronizes the HashMap. State persistence is retained using browser localStorage. The project contains dedicated real-time visualizers that render the exact internal state of all three data structures on an interactive dashboard, keeping the implementation educational, practical, and fully demonstratable.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 57 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (History tracking and back/forward state navigation are fundamental features of every modern web browser \(such as Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Microsoft Edge\). As users browse across websites, they expect instantaneous backtracking to previous pages, re-forwarding to undone pages, and rapid searching through past browsing sessions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (This project demonstrates the core engineering and algorithms behind these features through a fully functional application. When a user visits a website \(such as "google.com"\), the current page transitions onto the Back Stack while the Forward Stack is cleared. Clicking "Back" pops the previous URL from the Back Stack and pushes the current page into the Forward Stack. Simultaneously, the Doubly Linked List records the session in chronological order, while the HashMap indexes URL access frequencies and timestamps.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
